  "body": "Follow-up of #2586 (the headless `camcraft-sim check`).\n\n## Problem\n`camcraft-sim check --tools` reads a T map only (`T1=6:flute=20:holder=40x50`). A `.camtools` library file is refused by name, with exit 1.\n\nThere are two causes:\n- The `.camtools` reader is `parse_tool_library` in `crates/app/src/tool_library.rs`. It calls `project::tool_from_json`, which is also in `camcraft-app`. `camcraft-simview` must not depend on `camcraft-app` (#2574, answer 5).\n- A `.camtools` file states no T numbers. `LibraryTool.number` is `None` for this format. The check must not guess a T number from the row order, because a wrong tool gives a wrong verdict.\n\n## Proposal\n1. Move the world-free part of the `.camtools` reader (the row reader and the tool definition reader) to `camcraft-tools`. `camcraft-app` re-exports it.\n2. Give `check` a way to map each T number to a library tool by name, for example `--tools lib.camtools --tmap T1=\"EM-1000-4FL\",T3=\"DR-0600\"`.\n3. Keep the refusal for a T number that has no tool.\n\n## Done means\n`camcraft-sim check --tools <file.camtools> --tmap ...` judges the holder and fixture laws with the library's flute, shank and holder sizes. A test in `crates/simview/tests/check/` pins it.\n",
